[% META
    title        = "Immutable Image Deploy Plan (IMGDEP)"
    description  = "Replace the bind-mount + git-pull + docker-cp deploy model with a single immutable, git-sha-tagged container image as the only production artifact."
    roles        = "admin,developer"
    TemplateType = "Documentation"
    category     = "System / Infrastructure"
    page_version = "1.0"
    last_updated = "2026-08-04"
    site_specific = "false"
%]

<div class="container">

    <h1>Immutable Image Deploy Plan (IMGDEP)</h1>

    <div class="last-updated" style="color: var(--text-muted-color); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-color); padding-bottom: 8px; margin-bottom: 16px;">
        Last Updated: 2026-08-04 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Version: 1.0 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Status: <strong style="color: var(--warning-color);">DRAFT — awaiting review</strong>
    </div>

    <div id="toc" class="row" style="border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <div class="col-100">
            <strong style="color: var(--text-color);">Quick Navigation</strong><br>
            <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="#problem">1. The Problem</a>
            <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="#evidence">2. Evidence</a>
            <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="#rootcause">3. Root Cause</a>
            <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="#target">4. Target Model</a>
            <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="#tradeoffs">5. Trade-offs</a>
            <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="#phases">6. Phases</a>
            <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="#rollback">7. Rollback</a>
            <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="#decisions">8. Open Decisions</a>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2 id="problem">1. The Problem</h2>

    <p style="color: var(--text-color);">
        Production deploys are not reliably verifiable. After a deploy, the question
        <em>"is my code actually live?"</em> cannot be answered from any single source of truth.
        This has consumed multiple days of engineering time and has produced repeated
        false conclusions in both directions — believing prod was stale when it was current,
        and believing it was current when it was stale.
    </p>

    <p style="color: var(--text-color);">
        The observable symptom on 2026-08-04: a deploy served new code to production while
        <code>DEPLOY_STATUS.json</code> still reported a commit from six weeks earlier, and
        <code>deploy-logs/</code> received no new entry. Meanwhile <code>version.json</code> had been
        stamped with the new commit. Two deploy artifacts, written by the same script,
        disagreed with each other. The only way to establish the truth was to fetch production
        URLs and grep the returned HTML for database error strings.
    </p>

    <p><a href="#toc" style="color: var(--link-color);">↑ Back to Top</a></p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2 id="evidence">2. Evidence</h2>

    <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Value</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Deploy logs on disk</td>
                <td><strong>110</strong> (<code>deploy-logs/*.log</code>)</td>
                <td>Deploying is a frequent, high-friction, retry-heavy activity.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>Comserv/script/deploy.sh</code></td>
                <td><strong>1,658 lines</strong></td>
                <td>Deploy logic has grown to absorb the ambiguity of the artifact model.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Competing delivery mechanisms in one script</td>
                <td><strong>3</strong></td>
                <td>docker build/push/pull; <code>git pull</code> on the prod host (11 call sites); <code>docker cp</code> of host <code>lib/</code> into the running container.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Production bind mount</td>
                <td><code>./Comserv:/opt/comserv:ro</code> (<code>docker-compose.yml:145</code>)</td>
                <td>The container serves the host working directory, not the image contents.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Deploy script comment at <code>:268</code></td>
                <td>"Docker image alone does not include git pull."</td>
                <td>The artifact model is documented as insufficient, in the code itself.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Restart-loop mitigation at <code>:283</code></td>
                <td>Lib-tree SHA-256 stamp file</td>
                <td>Added because unconditional restarts were "bouncing production 6x/hour".</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Compose build context</td>
                <td><code>context: .</code> + <code>dockerfile: Dockerfile</code></td>
                <td><strong>No <code>Dockerfile</code> exists at repo root</strong> — it is at <code>Comserv/Dockerfile</code>. The prod build stanza cannot work as written.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Compose build target</td>
                <td><code>target: production</code></td>
                <td><strong>No stage named <code>production</code> exists.</strong> <code>Comserv/Dockerfile</code> declares only <code>builder</code>; the runtime stage is unnamed.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div style="border-left: 4px solid var(--warning-color); padding: 10px 14px; margin: 16px 0; background: var(--bg-alt-color);">
        <strong style="color: var(--text-color);">Key inference:</strong>
        <span style="color: var(--text-color);">
        The last two rows mean the <code>web-prod</code> service <em>cannot presently be built from
        compose at all</em>. Production therefore runs an image built by some other path, with the
        real application code supplied by the bind mount. This is why the image tag carries no
        information about what is running.
        </span>
    </div>

    <p><a href="#toc" style="color: var(--link-color);">↑ Back to Top</a></p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2 id="rootcause">3. Root Cause</h2>

    <p style="color: var(--text-color);">
        <strong>There is no deployment artifact.</strong> The container image is a Perl runtime
        wrapper; the application code arrives separately via bind mount, <code>git pull</code>, or
        <code>docker cp</code>. Because code identity is decoupled from image identity, no
        <code>docker</code> command can report what is running, and every "what is live?" question
        must be answered by out-of-band bookkeeping files that are written non-atomically and
        therefore drift.
    </p>

    <p style="color: var(--text-color);">
        Every persistent deploy defect is downstream of this one fact:
    </p>

    <ul style="color: var(--text-color);">
        <li><strong>Unverifiable deploys</strong> — no immutable identity to inspect.</li>
        <li><strong>Bookkeeping drift</strong> — <code>DEPLOY_STATUS.json</code> vs <code>version.json</code> vs <code>deploy-logs/</code> vs the container's own state are four independent writes; any can fail silently.</li>
        <li><strong>The lib-sync / restart loop</strong> — a workaround for the image not containing the code.</li>
        <li><strong>No real rollback</strong> — reverting means re-running git operations on the prod host, not re-pulling a known-good artifact.</li>
        <li><strong>Deploy-script growth</strong> — the 1,658 lines are almost entirely reconciliation logic between the three delivery paths.</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="color: var(--text-color);">
        We are paying the full complexity cost of containers while receiving none of their
        central guarantee: that a running container is a known, reproducible artifact.
    </p>

    <p><a href="#toc" style="color: var(--link-color);">↑ Back to Top</a></p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2 id="target">4. Target Model</h2>

    <p style="color: var(--text-color);">
        <strong>The image is the only artifact. It is immutable and tagged by git SHA.</strong>
    </p>

    <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Code enters the image at build time</td><td><code>COPY</code> only. No bind mount of application code in production.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tag by content, never by <code>:latest</code></td><td><code>comserv-web-prod:&lt;git-sha&gt;</code>. <code>:latest</code> may exist as a moving alias but is never what a deploy references.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Deploy is pull + up</td><td><code>docker pull &lt;sha&gt;</code> then <code>docker compose up -d</code>. No git operations on the production host.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Identity is queryable</td><td><code>docker inspect</code> returns the running SHA. This replaces <code>DEPLOY_STATUS.json</code>, <code>version.json</code>, and the lib-hash stamp file.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rollback is a tag change</td><td>Re-pull the previous SHA and restart. No reverse-migration of source on the host.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Refuse to deploy a dirty tree</td><td>A build from uncommitted code produces a SHA that does not exist in git. The build must fail loudly rather than auto-commit.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p style="color: var(--text-color);">
        Mounts that <em>remain</em> in production (these are data and config, not code, and are
        correctly external to the image): the secrets directory, the NFS data path, the
        whisper venv volume, and the CPAN cache volume.
    </p>

    <p><a href="#toc" style="color: var(--link-color);">↑ Back to Top</a></p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2 id="tradeoffs">5. Trade-offs (read before approving)</h2>

    <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>What we gain</th><th>What we give up</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>A deploy can be verified in one command.</td>
                <td><strong>No hot-editing <code>.pm</code> files on production.</strong> Today a file can be changed on the prod host and picked up by a restart. That stops.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Rollback becomes trivial and safe.</td>
                <td><strong>Every change requires a build.</strong> A one-line fix costs a full image build and push (minutes, not seconds).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Most of <code>deploy.sh</code> becomes deletable.</td>
                <td>Build infrastructure must be reliable, because there is no longer a manual bypass.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Bookkeeping files and their drift disappear.</td>
                <td>Registry storage grows — needs a tag retention policy.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div style="border-left: 4px solid var(--info-color); padding: 10px 14px; margin: 16px 0; background: var(--bg-alt-color);">
        <span style="color: var(--text-color);">
        The loss of hot-editing is the real cost and should not be minimised. The counter-argument
        is that hot-editing is precisely the mechanism that makes the current system unverifiable:
        it is the reason a running container's contents cannot be predicted from its tag.
        </span>
    </div>

    <p><a href="#toc" style="color: var(--link-color);">↑ Back to Top</a></p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2 id="phases">6. Phases</h2>

    <p style="color: var(--text-color);">
        Infrastructure first, then a gate, then application cutover — consistent with the
        project's standing deploy-work convention. <strong>No phase after Ph.0 begins until the
        preceding gate is demonstrated.</strong>
    </p>

    <h3 style="color: var(--text-color);">Ph.0 — Repair the build definition <span style="color: var(--danger-color);">(blocker)</span></h3>
    <ul style="color: var(--text-color);">
        <li>Name the runtime stage in <code>Comserv/Dockerfile</code> (<code>FROM ${BASE_IMAGE} AS production</code>).</li>
        <li>Correct <code>docker-compose.yml</code> <code>web-prod.build</code> to point at the real Dockerfile path and context.</li>
        <li>Audit <code>.dockerignore</code> so that <code>COPY . .</code> captures the application and excludes secrets, logs, and <code>blib/</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="color: var(--text-color);"><strong>GATE 0:</strong> <code>docker compose build web-prod</code> succeeds from a clean checkout, unassisted.</p>

    <h3 style="color: var(--text-color);">Ph.1 — Prove the image is self-sufficient</h3>
    <ul style="color: var(--text-color);">
        <li>Run the freshly built image locally with <strong>the code bind mount removed</strong> (data/secret mounts retained).</li>
        <li>Exercise the routes that previously exposed deploy failures: <code>/</code>, <code>/shop</code>, <code>/membership</code>, <code>/planning/daily</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="color: var(--text-color);"><strong>GATE 1:</strong> All four routes return HTTP 200 with no database or template error signatures, with no application code mounted from the host.</p>

    <h3 id="registry" style="color: var(--text-color);">Ph.1.5 — Stand up the LAN registry <span style="color: var(--danger-color);">(prerequisite for Ph.2/Ph.3)</span></h3>
    <p style="color: var(--text-color);">
        Decided 2026-08-04 (D1/D2): build locally on the workstation; push to a <strong>LAN-local</strong>
        registry; production pulls over the LAN. GitHub Actions is rejected — it adds a cloud round-trip
        to a LAN-internal problem. The registry is our own infrastructure: it must exist before a deploy
        can run, and its storage must be monitored. It is a portable Docker LAN service, consistent with
        the project convention.
    </p>
    <ul style="color: var(--text-color);">
        <li>Run <code>registry:2</code> on a LAN host (e.g. the NFS host or the workstation itself) on port <code>5000</code>; give it a named volume so tags persist across registry restarts.</li>
        <li>Because the registry is HTTP (not TLS), every Docker daemon that pulls from it must list it as an insecure registry (<code>/etc/docker/daemon.json</code> → <code>"insecure-registries": ["&lt;lan-host&gt;:5000"]</code>, then <code>systemctl restart docker</code>) on <strong>both</strong> the workstation and production1.</li>
        <li>Tag scheme: <code>&lt;lan-host&gt;:5000/comserv-web-prod:&lt;git-sha&gt;</code> and a floating <code>:latest</code> alias (never what a deploy references).</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="color: var(--text-color);"><strong>GATE 1.5:</strong> <code>docker push</code> from the workstation and <code>docker pull</code> from production1 both succeed against the LAN registry; <code>docker inspect</code> on a pulled image reports the pushed SHA tag.</p>

    <h3 style="color: var(--text-color);">Ph.2 — SHA tagging, provenance, and push</h3>
    <ul style="color: var(--text-color);">
        <li>Compute the SHA from the <em>committed</em> tree: <code>SHA=$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)</code>. <strong>Refuse to build on a dirty tree</strong> — a build from uncommitted code produces a SHA that does not exist in git, which is exactly the "which build is this?" ambiguity we are removing. Fail loudly instead of auto-committing.</li>
        <li>Build and tag: <code>docker build -t &lt;lan-host&gt;:5000/comserv-web-prod:$SHA -f Comserv/Dockerfile Comserv/</code> then <code>docker push &lt;lan-host&gt;:5000/comserv-web-prod:$SHA</code>.</li>
        <li>Embed the SHA as an OCI label (<code>--label com.comserv.build.git_sha=$SHA</code>) so it survives even without the tag.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="color: var(--text-color);"><strong>GATE 2:</strong> <code>docker inspect</code> on a running container reports a SHA that resolves to a real commit in <code>git log</code>; <code>docker pull</code> of that tag from production1 succeeds.</p>

    <h3 style="color: var(--text-color);">Ph.3 — Production cutover (pull + up, no git on prod)</h3>
    <ul style="color: var(--text-color);">
        <li>Remove <code>./Comserv:/opt/comserv:ro</code> from the <code>web-prod</code> service in <code>docker-compose.yml</code>.</li>
        <li>On production1: <code>docker pull &lt;lan-host&gt;:5000/comserv-web-prod:$SHA</code> then restart the service pinned to that tag. No <code>git pull</code>, no <code>docker cp</code>.</li>
        <li>Verify by <code>docker inspect</code> (the SHA must match the workstation build) <em>and</em> by symptom probe (the method that settled the 2026-08-04 question): fetch the route groups and grep for error signatures.</li>
        <li>Supply <code>db_config.json</code> as an explicit runtime mount (not the whole code tree) until the bootstrap retirement (IMGSEC #1884) lands.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="color: var(--text-color);"><strong>GATE 3:</strong> Production1 server header reports the same Git SHA as the workstation (<code>main@&lt;sha&gt;</code>); a deliberate rollback to the prior SHA (re-pull + restart) succeeds and is verified. <strong>Note:</strong> as of 2026-08-04 production1 was 3 days stale on a divergent commit (<code>c58120ed</code> vs workstation <code>850bd3fb</code>) — GATE 3 is not met until both nodes agree.</p>

    <h3 style="color: var(--text-color);">Ph.4 — Retire the superseded mechanisms</h3>
    <ul style="color: var(--text-color);">
        <li>Delete <code>sync_host_app_lib()</code> and the lib-hash stamp file.</li>
        <li>Remove <code>git pull</code> from the production path.</li>
        <li>Retire <code>DEPLOY_STATUS.json</code> and <code>version.json</code> as deploy-truth sources; <code>docker inspect</code> replaces both.</li>
        <li>Reduce <code>deploy.sh</code> to build / push / pull / up / rollback.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="color: var(--text-color);"><strong>GATE 4:</strong> Two consecutive deploys performed with no manual intervention and no reconciliation step.</p>

    <p><a href="#toc" style="color: var(--link-color);">↑ Back to Top</a></p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2 id="rollback">7. Rollback &amp; Safety</h2>

    <ul style="color: var(--text-color);">
        <li>Phases 0–2 are entirely non-destructive: they add a correct build path beside the existing one and change nothing about how production currently runs.</li>
        <li>The single irreversible-feeling step is Ph.3 (removing the code bind mount). Its rollback is to restore one line in <code>docker-compose.yml</code> and restart.</li>
        <li>The previous image SHA remains pullable, so Ph.3 has a second, artifact-level rollback path independent of the compose file.</li>
        <li>Retirement of old mechanisms (Ph.4) happens <em>only after</em> two clean deploys, per gate 4.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><a href="#toc" style="color: var(--link-color);">↑ Back to Top</a></p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <h2 id="decisions">8. Open Decisions</h2>

    <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Decision</th><th>Resolution</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>D1</td>
                <td>Where do builds run?</td>
                <td><strong style="color: var(--success-color);">DECIDED 2026-08-04 — build locally on the workstation.</strong>
                    The existing local-build → push → pull path already works and is retained.
                    GitHub Actions is <em>rejected</em>: it adds a cloud dependency, a second credential
                    system, and slower shared-runner builds to solve a problem we do not have. The
                    unreliability was never about where the image was built — it was that the image
                    was not the artifact being deployed. <strong>Action:</strong> delete
                    <code>.github/workflows/</code> so the half-built path cannot be mistaken for a
                    supported one.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>D2</td>
                <td>Registry</td>
                <td><strong style="color: var(--success-color);">DECIDED 2026-08-04 — LAN-local registry.</strong>
                    Nothing needs to leave the LAN. A <code>registry:2</code> container on a LAN host
                    replaces the Docker Hub round-trip: build on the workstation, push to
                    <code>&lt;lan-host&gt;:5000/comserv-web-prod:&lt;sha&gt;</code>, pull from production over
                    the LAN. Faster, works during an internet outage, no external account, no rate
                    limits. Consistent with the project convention of running heavy tools as portable
                    Docker LAN services. <strong>Accepted cost:</strong> the registry is now our own
                    infrastructure — it must exist before a deploy can run, and its storage must be
                    monitored.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>D3</td>
                <td>Emergency hot-fix policy</td>
                <td><strong style="color: var(--warning-color);">DEFERRED — to be decided on Ph.1 evidence.</strong>
                    The existing hot-edit fallback was introduced to keep production available despite
                    unreliable deploys. Note the circularity: the fallback exists because deploys are
                    unreliable, and deploys are unreliable because the fallback bypasses the artifact
                    model. Note also that on 2026-08-04 the fallback did not keep production
                    <em>correct</em> — five real errors ran live for six weeks and were undetectable
                    precisely because running code could not be tied to a build. Ph.1 answers this
                    empirically: run the image with the code mount removed and observe whether it
                    stands up. A middle option remains available — retain the bind mount line in the
                    production compose file but commented out, as a documented break-glass that
                    requires a deliberate edit and leaves a trace.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>D4</td>
                <td>Tag scheme and retention</td>
                <td><strong style="color: var(--success-color);">DECIDED 2026-08-04 — tag = git commit SHA; keep ~10.</strong>
                    "SHA" here means simply the git commit id (e.g. <code>850bd3fb</code>) — the same
                    string <code>git log</code> prints. This is not a new mechanism: builds were
                    <em>already</em> being tagged to bridge Docker labels to build identity, which was
                    the right instinct. The only change is that the tag becomes the commit and the code
                    lives inside the tagged image, so the label cannot lie. Today a tag can read
                    <code>850bd3fb</code> while the bind mount serves something else — that gap is what
                    the tagging was trying to close and could not. Retain the last ~10 SHA tags plus any
                    currently deployed; storage is cheap on a LAN registry.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="ha" style="color: var(--text-color);">Why this is a prerequisite for HA / Kubernetes</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--text-color);">
        When an error surfaces on <code>production1</code> rather than another node, the question
        <em>"which build is this node running?"</em> must have a one-command answer <strong>per
        node</strong>. Under the bind-mount model every node can silently diverge, because node
        identity is a function of that host's working directory rather than of the image it was
        given. Kubernetes cannot schedule a pod without naming an exact image, so the immutable-image
        model is an <em>assumption</em> of the platform, not an option within it. Completing this plan
        is therefore a precondition for the planned multi-node high-availability and k8s work — not a
        detour from it.
    </p>

    <h3 id="creds" style="color: var(--text-color);">Credential handling and the db_config.json retirement</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--text-color);">
        This plan removes the application-<em>code</em> bind mount, but it must not recreate the
        credential-leak class it is meant to kill. Two facts, both tracked under project
        <a href="/project/details?project_id=239" style="color: var(--link-color);">IMGSEC (#239)</a>:
    </p>
    <ul style="color: var(--text-color);">
        <li><strong>Image-layer secrets are now excluded</strong> — <code>Comserv/.dockerignore</code>
            (Ph.0) blocks <code>db_config.json</code>, <code>*.key</code>, <code>*.pem</code>,
            <code>id_rsa*</code>, <code>ssh_credentials.json</code>, <code>.env*</code>, and
            <code>**/secrets/</code> from <code>COPY . .</code>. gitleaks guards git; this guards images;
            the two are not yet cross-checked (see Ph.4 / IMGSEC #1883).</li>
        <li><strong><code>db_config.json</code> was a bootstrap-only source by original design.</strong>
            It was to load initial credentials until the env-var / K8s Secrets system was in place, after
            which it was to be <strong>discontinued</strong>. That retirement never completed. Under this
            plan, during Ph.3 the file is supplied as an explicit runtime mount (not the whole code tree);
            in the end-state (IMGSEC #1884) it is removed entirely once env/K8s credential loading is the
            verified primary. The immutable-image model makes that retirement enforceable: with no code
            bind mount, the file's presence is a deliberate, reviewable compose entry — not an accident of
            <code>COPY . .</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="livedefects" style="color: var(--text-color);">Known live defects (discovered 2026-08-04, tracked under IMGSEC)</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--text-color);">
        Server-header evidence on 2026-08-04 exposed two independent production defects that the deploy
        rework must not mask. They are owned as IMGSEC todos, not folded silently into the cutover:
    </p>
    <ul style="color: var(--text-color);">
        <li><strong>Production drift (IMGSEC #1885):</strong> production1 (computersystemconsulting.ca)
            reported <code>Build: 2026-08-01 02:24</code> / <code>Git: main@c58120ed+local</code> while the
            workstation reported <code>Build: 2026-08-04 16:10</code> / <code>Git: main@850bd3fb+local</code>.
            Two nodes, two different "latest" states. GATE 3 of this plan is not met until both nodes
            report the same SHA.</li>
        <li><strong>Forager unresolved on prod (IMGSEC #1886):</strong> production1 reports
            <code>DBForager(Forager)@Unknown</code> while the workstation resolves
            <code>Forager@172.30.161.222</code>. A separate live defect (not the image-leak class), likely
            the cause of prod-only Forager errors. Diagnose DNS / ZeroTier reachability between hosts
            before assuming it is fixed by the image cutover.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><a href="#toc" style="color: var(--link-color);">↑ Back to Top</a></p>

